// ora-mod-gl — generated by tools/merge.js: src/ora-mod-gl-*.js plus matching questions from the Oracle role banks (tools/modules.js).
// Oracle Modules — General Ledger deep dive (part 1: Fusion GL). Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D']
QB['ora-mod-gl'] = (QB['ora-mod-gl'] || []).concat([
['Fusion GL', 'Walk me through the key components you set up for a ledger in Fusion GL.',
`- **Chart of accounts** structure and instance (segments, value sets, segment labels: primary balancing, cost center, natural account, intercompany).
- **Accounting calendar** (period type, adjusting period for year-end).
- **Ledger currency** and **accounting method** (SLA).
- **Legal entities** and **balancing segment value assignments**.
- **Ledger options**: retained earnings account, suspense, rounding, cumulative translation adjustment (CTA), net income account, entered currency balancing, journal approval, intercompany balancing, revaluation/translation defaults, average balance processing.
- **Reporting currencies** / **secondary ledgers**.
- **Ledger sets** and **data access sets**.
- **Account hierarchies** published to the **GL balances cube**.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'What is the GL balances cube (Essbase) and why does it matter?',
`- Each **chart of accounts + calendar** combination gets an **Essbase balances cube** updated in real time when journals post.
- Powers **Smart View**, **Financial Reporting**, **Account Monitor/Inspector**, allocations (Calculation Manager) and **multidimensional analysis**.
- Dimensions include accounting period, ledger, each COA segment, currency, currency type, scenario (actual, budget), balance amount (period/QTD/YTD) and amount type.
- **Account hierarchies (trees)** must be **published** to the cube for roll-up reporting.
- If the cube is out of sync (e.g. after hierarchy changes), run **Create Cubes / Rebuild Dimension Members / Refresh** processes.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'How does year-end close work in Fusion GL?',
`- Post all adjustments, including an **adjusting period** (e.g. "Adj-24") if the calendar has one.
- Run final revaluation/translation, intercompany and allocations; reconcile.
- **Open the first period of the new year** — GL automatically rolls **P&L balances into the retained earnings account** (defined per ledger, by balancing segment) and carries balance sheet balances forward. No closing journal is needed.
- If late adjustments hit the prior year after the new year is open, **reopen** the prior period, post, and re-open the next year period to refresh retained earnings.
- Close/permanently close after audit.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'What are the journal reversal options in Fusion GL?',
`- **Reversal period**: next period, same period, or a specific period; **reversal date** for average-balance ledgers.
- **Reversal method**: **switch debit/credit** or **change sign** (negative amounts).
- Set by **journal category** (e.g. accruals auto-reverse) via **Manage Journal Reversal Criteria** and **AutoReverse** scheduling.
- Manual reversal from the journal page.
- Reversals keep a link to the original for audit.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'Explain reporting currency conversion levels: subledger, journal and balance.',
`- **Balance level**: translates period-end balances (like translation) — cheapest; no transaction detail in the reporting currency.
- **Journal level**: every posted GL journal is converted and posted to the reporting currency.
- **Subledger level**: subledger accounting creates entries directly in the reporting currency (most detail, highest volume) — needed when subledger-level reporting in the second currency matters.
- Choose based on detail required, volume and performance. Same rules apply to secondary ledgers.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'What are journal conversion rules and COA mappings for secondary ledgers?',
`- **Journal conversion rules** decide which journals (by source/category) are copied from the primary to the secondary/reporting ledger at journal level — e.g. exclude manual GAAP-specific adjustments that should only exist in one ledger.
- **Chart of accounts mapping** converts primary COA combinations into the secondary ledger's COA (segment rules or account rules), needed when the secondary ledger uses a **different COA** (e.g. a local statutory COA).
- Both are set on the **primary-to-secondary ledger relationship**.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'How do you set up revaluation in Fusion GL?',
`- **Define revaluation**: accounts to revalue (filters on account ranges — usually monetary balance sheet accounts: AR, AP, cash, intercompany, loans in foreign currency), rate type (e.g. period-end Corporate), unrealized gain and loss accounts, and whether to revalue **P&L accounts** (PTD/YTD).
- Run per ledger (or ledger set) per period; post the generated journal.
- Revaluation journals usually **auto-reverse** next period.
- Primary BSV can be kept from the source account for gain/loss.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'How does translation work in Fusion GL and where does CTA go?',
`- Converts a ledger's **balances** into another currency for reporting/consolidation (translated balances, not journals).
- **Balance sheet** at period-end rate; **income statement** at average (PTD) rate; **equity/historical accounts** at **historical rates or amounts** (defined via Manage Historical Rates).
- The balancing difference goes to the **cumulative translation adjustment (CTA)** account defined on the ledger.
- Run after revaluation and all postings; rerun after late adjustments.
- Many groups now translate in **FCCS** instead and only use GL translation for local needs.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'Explain how intercompany balancing works when a journal crosses legal entities.',
`- If a journal has lines for **different primary balancing segment values** (legal entities), GL/SLA creates **intercompany receivable/payable** lines automatically.
- Rules are defined in **Manage Intercompany Balancing Rules** (by ledger, legal entity, source/category, chart of accounts) — e.g. "use account 1810 for IC receivable with BSV of the trading partner in the intercompany segment".
- **Clearing company** options if many entities are on one side (use a clearing BSV or a default).
- **Intracompany** balancing handles BSVs within the same legal entity.
- Results feed intercompany reconciliation and eliminations.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'What is a suspense account and when would you enable it?',
`- If a journal is **out of balance**, GL can post the difference to a **suspense account** instead of rejecting it.
- Enabled per ledger (with optional suspense by source/category).
- Most companies **disable** it for manual journals and integrations — better to fix the source.
- If used, reconcile and clear suspense every period; it's an audit flag.`, 'B'],

['Fusion GL', 'How do you load budgets into Fusion GL?',
`- **Budget scenarios** in the balances cube (Scenario dimension members).
- Load via **spreadsheet (Smart View / budget upload)** or **FBDI** budget template, by account and period.
- Or integrate from **EPM Planning** (preferred for real planning processes).
- Used for **budget vs actual** reports in Financial Reporting/Smart View.
- **Budgetary control** budgets are separate (control budgets) and enforce spending limits.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'What are statistical journals and statistical accounts used for?',
`- Record **non-monetary quantities** — headcount, square footage, units produced — in the ledger (STAT currency or statistical accounts).
- Used as **bases for allocations** (rent by square feet, IT cost by headcount) and KPIs (cost per head).
- Loaded each period by journal/spreadsheet or integration.`, 'B'],

['Fusion GL', 'How do allocations work in Fusion GL with Calculation Manager?',
`- **Allocation rules** built in **Calculation Manager** against the balances cube: **source** (pool to allocate), **basis** (driver — statistical or monetary), **target** (receiving accounts/cost centers), **offset** (credit back).
- **Rule sets** run several rules in sequence (step-down allocations).
- **Runtime prompts** (period, ledger) make rules reusable.
- **Generate General Ledger Allocations** creates journals, which then post (optionally auto-post).
- Test with known numbers; document drivers for audit.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'How do you troubleshoot a Smart View report that doesn\'t match the trial balance?',
`- Check **POV**: ledger, period, currency, **currency type** (entered vs total), **amount type** (PTD/YTD), scenario, balance type.
- Were journals **posted** after the last cube update? (Cube updates on posting — check for failed **Update Balances Cube** processes.)
- **Hierarchy version**: tree changed but not republished.
- **Data access** differences between users.
- Compare the trial balance report with the same parameters; drill into Account Inspector.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'Explain ledger sets and when you\'d use them.',
`- A **ledger set** groups ledgers sharing the **same COA and calendar** (currencies can differ).
- Lets you **open/close periods**, run **revaluation/translation/allocations**, report and grant **data access** across many ledgers at once.
- Typical: all European ledgers in one set; a global set for consolidated reporting in GL.`, 'B'],

['Fusion GL', 'What is a data access set and how does it differ from segment value security?',
`- **Data access set**: which **ledgers/ledger sets** and which **primary balancing segment values** (or management segment values) a user can access, read-only or read/write — controls journal entry, inquiry and period management.
- **Segment value security**: restricts **any segment's values** (e.g. cost centers) in entry and reporting.
- Typical design: data access sets for legal-entity scope; segment security only where truly needed (performance and complexity).`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'How does journal import from external systems work, and how do you make it robust?',
`- **FBDI journal import** (JournalImportTemplate) or **ERP Integration Service** (importBulkData) from OIC; also REST for small volumes.
- Process: load to **GL_INTERFACE** → **Import Journals** (by ledger, source, group ID) → optional **AutoPost**.
- Robustness: a dedicated **journal source** per system, **group IDs** per batch, **cross-validation** before sending, **control totals**, callbacks/alerts on errors, idempotency to avoid double loads, reconciliation reports per source.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'What is the difference between Account Monitor, Account Inspector and Financial Reports?',
`- **Account Monitor**: watch key account balances (with thresholds) on the GL dashboard; drill down.
- **Account Inspector**: ad-hoc pivot/drill on the balances cube by any segment and hierarchy, down to journals and subledger detail.
- **Financial Reporting / Smart View**: formatted statements and Excel-based analysis.
- All read the same Essbase cube.`, 'B'],

['Fusion GL', 'How do you set up journal approval and what are common issues?',
`- Enable at **ledger** and **journal source** level; rules in **BPM (Manage Journal Approval Rules)** — e.g. by maximum line amount, ledger, category, approval hierarchy (supervisor/position/approval groups).
- **Issues**: missing approvers (terminated supervisors), self-approval, approvals routed for subledger sources unintentionally, slow approvals at close.
- **Fixes**: fallback approvers, delegation, exclude subledger sources, monitor pending approvals during close.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'How do you handle a mid-year change to the chart of accounts, such as splitting a cost center?',
`- Create the new value(s), update **hierarchies**, publish trees.
- Update **defaults**: HCM assignments, supplier/customer defaults, SLA mapping sets, allocations, cross-validation rules, security.
- **Reclass** existing balances with a journal (or keep history and start fresh — agree with FP&A).
- End-date the old value (disable after a transition).
- Re-test integrations and reports; communicate the effective date.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'What GL reports and checks are part of your month-end close?',
`- **Trial balance** by ledger/legal entity.
- **Unposted journals** and **journals pending approval**.
- **Subledger to GL reconciliations** (AP, AR, FA, Inventory).
- **Intercompany reconciliation**.
- **Suspense and clearing account** balances.
- **Revaluation/translation** results; CTA movement.
- **Variance analysis** vs prior period/budget.
- **Journal entry review** for manual/top-side entries.`, 'B'],

['Fusion GL', 'How would you design the GL for a company reporting under IFRS at group level and US GAAP for a US parent filing?',
`- Pick the **primary ledger GAAP** as the one with most transactions/legal requirement in each entity (often local GAAP or IFRS).
- **Secondary ledgers** for the other GAAP: **subledger-level** if many transactions differ (leases, revenue, assets); **adjustment-only** if few.
- **Fixed Assets**: separate corporate books per GAAP linked to each ledger.
- **Ledger sets** per GAAP for close and reporting.
- **FCCS** with the **Multi-GAAP** dimension (or separate scenarios) for consolidated IFRS and US GAAP.
- Document **GAAP differences** and the accounts used for adjustments.`, 'D'],

['Fusion GL', 'What is an adjusting period and when do you use it?',
`- An extra calendar period that overlaps the year-end date (e.g. "Adj-24" dated Dec 31) used for **audit and year-end adjustments** without reopening December.
- Balances roll into year-end reporting; retained earnings are recalculated when the new year is opened.
- Keeps December's operational results separate from audit adjustments.`, 'B'],

['Behavioral', 'Tell me about a difficult GL close issue you resolved as a contractor.',
`Structure (STAR), keep it specific:
- **Situation**: e.g. intercompany out of balance by $1.2M across 8 entities on day 3 of close.
- **Task**: find and fix before the consolidation deadline.
- **Action**: ran IC reconciliation by trading partner, traced to manual journals with the wrong intercompany segment and FX timing differences; corrected entries; added an IC segment cross-validation rule and a weekly IC matching review.
- **Result**: close met; IC differences dropped to near zero the following months.`, 'B'],

['Behavioral', 'As a GL contractor, how do you get productive in the first two weeks?',
`- **Read**: enterprise structure, COA, ledger setup, close calendar, recent audit findings, open tickets.
- **Shadow** a month-end close.
- **Map** integrations and journal sources feeding GL.
- **Meet** the controller, GL accountants, FP&A, IT/support.
- **Quick win**: fix a recurring close pain point.
- Keep a running **issues and recommendations log**.`, 'B']
]);

// Oracle Modules — General Ledger deep dive (part 2: IFRS and multi-GAAP). Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D']
QB['ora-mod-gl'] = (QB['ora-mod-gl'] || []).concat([
['IFRS', 'What are the key differences between IFRS and US GAAP that affect the General Ledger?',
`- **Inventory**: IFRS (IAS 2) prohibits **LIFO**; write-downs can be **reversed**. US GAAP allows LIFO; no reversal.
- **PP&E**: IFRS (IAS 16) allows the **revaluation model** and requires **component depreciation**. US GAAP: cost model only.
- **Impairment**: IFRS (IAS 36) one-step test at **CGU** level, **reversals allowed** (except goodwill). US GAAP: two-step for long-lived assets, no reversals.
- **Development costs**: capitalized under IFRS (IAS 38) when criteria are met; expensed under US GAAP (except certain software).
- **Leases**: IFRS 16 single lessee model; ASC 842 finance vs operating.
- **Provisions**: IFRS "probable" = more likely than not, best estimate (midpoint of a range); US GAAP higher threshold, low end of a range.
- **Financial instruments**: IFRS 9 expected credit loss (3 stages) vs US GAAP CECL (lifetime from day one).
- **Consolidation**: IFRS 10 single control model; US GAAP voting interest + VIE models.
Each difference becomes an **accounting method/secondary ledger or adjustment** decision in Oracle.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'How do you support IFRS and local GAAP in Oracle Fusion?',
`- **Primary ledger** in one GAAP, **secondary ledger** in the other:
- **Subledger level**: SLA creates entries for both GAAPs from the same transaction using **different accounting methods** — for systematic differences.
- **Journal/adjustment-only level**: copy GL journals (with conversion rules) and post GAAP-only adjustments in the secondary ledger.
- **Fixed Assets**: separate corporate books (or depreciation differences) per GAAP.
- **Lease Accounting**: IFRS 16 vs ASC 842 treatments per ledger.
- **COA mapping** when the statutory COA differs.
- Reporting via **ledger sets** and FCCS Multi-GAAP.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'Explain IFRS 16 lease accounting and how it differs from ASC 842.',
`- **IFRS 16 (lessee)**: virtually all leases on balance sheet — **right-of-use asset** and **lease liability** (PV of payments). P&L shows **depreciation + interest** (front-loaded expense). Exemptions: **short-term** (≤12 months) and **low-value** assets.
- **ASC 842**: also on balance sheet, but **dual model**: **finance leases** (like IFRS 16) and **operating leases** with a **single straight-line lease cost**. No low-value exemption.
- **Oracle**: Fusion **Lease Accounting** calculates ROU and liability, amortization schedules, and accounting per ledger/GAAP; payments flow to AP.
- Interview point: EBITDA is higher under IFRS 16 because lease cost moves below EBITDA (depreciation/interest).`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'Explain IAS 21 foreign currency: functional currency, transactions and translation.',
`- **Functional currency**: currency of the primary economic environment (sales prices, costs, financing) — determined per entity.
- **Transactions** in foreign currency: recorded at spot rate; **monetary items** retranslated at closing rate each period (**exchange differences in P&L**); non-monetary items at historical rate.
- **Translation to presentation currency**: assets/liabilities at **closing rate**, income/expenses at **transaction (average) rates**, differences to **OCI (foreign currency translation reserve)**; recycled to P&L on disposal of the foreign operation.
- **Oracle**: revaluation in GL for monetary items; translation in GL (CTA) or FCCS.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'What is IAS 29 hyperinflation and how would you handle it in Oracle?',
`- For entities whose functional currency is **hyperinflationary** (e.g. cumulative 3-year inflation near 100%, as in Argentina or Türkiye in recent years), financial statements are **restated** using a general price index; non-monetary items are indexed; gain/loss on net monetary position goes to P&L; then translated at the **closing rate** (including P&L).
- **Oracle**: often handled with a **separate ledger/secondary ledger or adjustment process** and index-based adjustments (spreadsheets or EPM), with translation in FCCS using closing rates for P&L.
- Mention that the hyperinflation list should be checked each reporting period.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'What is IFRS 18 and how could it affect ERP and reporting?',
`- **IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements** replaces IAS 1, effective for annual periods beginning on or after **1 January 2027** (with comparatives restated).
- **New P&L structure**: income and expenses classified into **operating, investing, financing** (plus income taxes and discontinued operations) categories, with required subtotals **operating profit** and **profit before financing and income taxes**.
- **Management-defined performance measures (MPMs)** disclosed and reconciled in the notes.
- More guidance on **aggregation/disaggregation**.
- **ERP impact**: COA attributes or hierarchies to classify accounts into the new categories; FX and interest classification reviews; reporting templates (FR/FCCS/Narrative Reporting) redesigned; comparatives need restated mapping.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'How are provisions and contingent liabilities treated under IAS 37?',
`- **Provision** recognized when: present obligation from a past event, **probable outflow** (more likely than not), and a **reliable estimate**.
- Measured at the **best estimate** (expected value for large populations; most likely outcome for single obligations; midpoint of a range when all points are equally likely); discounted if material.
- **Contingent liabilities**: disclosed, not recognized (unless remote). **Contingent assets**: disclosed when probable, recognized when virtually certain.
- **Onerous contracts** and **restructuring** provisions have specific rules.
- GL: provision accounts, monthly review and true-ups, audit documentation.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'Explain IAS 36 impairment testing.',
`- Assess **indicators** at each reporting date; **goodwill and indefinite-life intangibles** tested at least annually.
- **Recoverable amount** = higher of **fair value less costs of disposal** and **value in use** (discounted cash flows).
- Test at **cash-generating unit (CGU)** level; goodwill allocated to CGUs.
- Loss = carrying amount − recoverable amount; allocated to goodwill first, then other assets pro rata.
- **Reversals** allowed (not for goodwill) up to the carrying amount without the impairment.
- In Oracle: **Assets impairment** functionality or journals; FCCS for goodwill at group level.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'What is component depreciation under IAS 16 and how do you set it up in Oracle Assets?',
`- Significant parts of an asset with **different useful lives** are depreciated separately (e.g. aircraft body vs engines vs interiors; building structure vs roof vs HVAC).
- In Oracle Assets: separate **assets (components)** linked via **parent-child/asset hierarchy**, each with its own category and life; or use separate asset lines.
- US GAAP books may keep a single asset — handle via separate corporate books per GAAP.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'How does the IAS 16 revaluation model work and how would you account for it?',
`- PP&E class carried at **fair value** at revaluation date less subsequent depreciation/impairment; revaluations regular enough that carrying amount doesn't differ materially from fair value.
- **Increase** → OCI (revaluation surplus) unless reversing a previous decrease in P&L.
- **Decrease** → P&L, unless reversing a previous surplus (then OCI).
- Surplus can be transferred to retained earnings as the asset is used/disposed.
- Oracle Assets supports **revaluation** (with options to revalue accumulated depreciation, amortize reserve).`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'Explain IFRS 9 expected credit loss for receivables.',
`- **ECL model** replaces incurred loss: recognize expected losses from the start.
- **Simplified approach** for trade receivables/contract assets: **lifetime ECL** using a **provision matrix** (loss rates by ageing bucket adjusted for forward-looking information).
- **General approach** (loans): Stage 1 12-month ECL; Stage 2 lifetime ECL after significant increase in credit risk; Stage 3 credit-impaired.
- **Oracle**: AR ageing feeds an allowance calculation (often in spreadsheets/EPM) posted to GL; document the methodology.
- US GAAP CECL: lifetime losses from day one for all in-scope assets.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'What does IFRS 10 say about control and consolidation?',
`- An investor **controls** an investee when it has: **power** over relevant activities, **exposure to variable returns**, and the **ability to use its power** to affect those returns.
- Control (not just >50% votes) drives consolidation — includes de facto control, potential voting rights, structured entities.
- **Non-controlling interests (NCI)** presented in equity.
- Changes in ownership without loss of control → equity transactions; loss of control → remeasure retained interest, gain/loss in P&L.
- **Associates** (IAS 28, significant influence) → equity method; **joint ventures** (IFRS 11) → equity method; joint operations → share of assets/liabilities.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'How does IAS 12 deferred tax work and how do you support it from the GL?',
`- Deferred tax on **temporary differences** between carrying amounts and tax bases (e.g. depreciation differences, provisions, leases).
- **DTA** recognized to the extent future taxable profit is **probable**; **DTL** generally recognized.
- Tax rates enacted or substantively enacted.
- **GL support**: tax book vs corporate book in Assets, account attributes for tax-relevant accounts, reports by entity; often calculated in **Tax Reporting (TRCS)** or tax provision tools and booked as journals.
- **Pillar Two** (global minimum tax) adds data needs and IAS 12 disclosures.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'What are the main IFRS vs US GAAP differences in cash flow statements?',
`- **IAS 7 (current)**: choice of classification for **interest and dividends** paid/received (operating, investing or financing).
- **IFRS 18 amendments to IAS 7** (from 2027): for most entities, **interest paid → financing**, **interest and dividends received → investing**, and the **indirect method starts from operating profit**.
- **US GAAP**: interest paid and received, and dividends received → **operating**; dividends paid → financing.
- **ERP impact**: account mapping/hierarchies for the cash flow, movement accounts or FCCS **Movement dimension**.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'What is IFRS 1 and when does it matter in an ERP implementation?',
`- **IFRS 1 First-time Adoption**: rules for a company moving to IFRS — opening IFRS balance sheet at the transition date, retrospective application with **optional exemptions** (deemed cost for PP&E, cumulative translation differences reset to zero, business combinations) and mandatory exceptions.
- **ERP**: converting balances to IFRS at transition (secondary ledger or adjustment ledger), comparative periods, mapping of accounts, documentation of elections.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'How are borrowing costs and investment property treated under IFRS?',
`- **IAS 23**: borrowing costs directly attributable to a **qualifying asset** (takes substantial time to get ready) must be **capitalized**. In Oracle: capitalize interest to CIP/assets via projects or journals.
- **IAS 40 investment property**: choose **fair value model** (changes in P&L, no depreciation) or **cost model** (with fair value disclosure). US GAAP has no separate investment property model (cost) except for investment companies.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'What is IFRS 15 and how do you support it in Oracle?',
`- **Five steps**: identify the contract → performance obligations → transaction price (including variable consideration) → allocate by relative **standalone selling price** → recognize revenue when/as obligations are satisfied.
- Largely converged with **ASC 606**; some differences (e.g. some practical expedients, licenses, onerous contract guidance in IAS 37).
- **Oracle**: **Revenue Management** (contracts, POs, SSP, allocation, recognition) integrated with OM/AR/Projects; accounting via SLA to GL; contract assets/liabilities.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'What disclosures does IFRS 8 operating segments require, and what does it mean for the COA?',
`- Segments based on the **management approach** — what the chief operating decision maker (CODM) reviews.
- Disclose segment profit/loss, assets (if reviewed), reconciliations to totals, entity-wide info (products, geographies, major customers).
- **COA/ERP**: a segment (business unit/product line) or **document splitting/balancing by segment** so profit (and possibly balance sheet) can be reported by segment; hierarchies aligned with management reporting.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'How do you keep IFRS and US GAAP books reconciled?',
`- **GAAP difference register**: each difference, accounts, amounts, owners.
- **Reconciliation reports**: equity and net income bridge between the two ledgers/scenarios each period.
- **Controls** over adjustment-only entries (approval, documentation).
- **Automate** recurring differences (SLA rules, FA books, lease accounting).
- **FCCS Multi-GAAP** shows the adjustments separately and totals both views.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'A client moving from US GAAP to IFRS asks what changes in their ERP. What do you tell them?',
`- **Policy decisions first** (PP&E model, components, development costs, leases, provisions, ECL, inventory costing without LIFO).
- **Ledger strategy** (primary/secondary) and FA book design.
- **SLA rule changes** for differing treatments.
- **COA/hierarchies** for new presentation (and IFRS 18 from 2027).
- **Opening balance** conversion (IFRS 1) and comparatives.
- **Reporting** templates, FCCS rules, training for accountants.`, 'D'],

['IFRS', 'Which IFRS standards have changed recently or are coming that a GL consultant should watch?',
`- **IFRS 18** (presentation and disclosure) — effective 1 January 2027, with restated comparatives.
- **IFRS 19** (subsidiaries without public accountability: reduced disclosures) — effective 1 January 2027.
- **IAS 12 Pillar Two** amendments (global minimum tax — temporary exception for deferred taxes plus disclosures).
- **IAS 21 lack of exchangeability** amendments (effective 2025).
- **IFRS 9/IFRS 7** amendments on classification and measurement (effective 2026).
Tip: say you check the IASB work plan and your client's auditor updates.`, 'D']
]);

// Oracle Modules — General Ledger deep dive (part 3: FCCS consolidation and close). Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D']
QB['ora-mod-gl'] = (QB['ora-mod-gl'] || []).concat([
['FCCS', 'What is Oracle FCCS and how does it fit with Fusion GL?',
`- **Financial Consolidation and Close (FCCS)** is Oracle's cloud **EPM** application for **group consolidation and close**: currency translation, intercompany eliminations, ownership/NCI, consolidation journals, cash flow, and close task management.
- **Fusion GL** remains the book of record for each ledger; FCCS receives **trial balances** (by entity, account, period, currency, often with intercompany partner and movement) via **Data Integration**, with **drill-through** back to Fusion.
- Reporting via **Financial Reports, Smart View and Narrative Reporting**.`, 'B'],

['FCCS', 'What are the standard dimensions in FCCS?',
`- **Account**, **Entity**, **Period**, **Years**, **Scenario** (Actual, Budget…), **View** (Periodic, YTD, QTD), **Currency**, **Consolidation**, **Intercompany**, **Data Source**, **Movement**.
- Optional **Multi-GAAP** dimension and **custom dimensions** (e.g. Product, Cost Center) — limited in number, so choose carefully.
- **Consolidation** dimension tracks the stages (entity input, proportion, elimination, contribution); **Data Source** tracks where data came from (data input, journals, supplemental data, calculations).`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'Walk me through the FCCS consolidation process.',
`- **Load** trial balances (and supplemental data) for each entity.
- **Calculate** entity-level rules (configurable calculations).
- **Translate** to parent currency using **average/ending rates** and historical overrides; **FX/CTA** calculated through the Movement dimension.
- **Proportionalize** by ownership/consolidation method.
- **Eliminate** intercompany balances and investments (equity elimination) at the first common parent.
- **Contribute** results to the parent; repeat up the hierarchy.
- **Journals** for top-side adjustments; **approvals** through the approval unit hierarchy.
- **Review** calculation status (OK, Impacted, System Changed) and reports.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'How does intercompany elimination work in FCCS?',
`- Intercompany balances are loaded with the **Intercompany (partner) dimension** populated (from the GL intercompany segment).
- **Intercompany matching reports** compare receivable vs payable (and revenue vs expense) between partner pairs, with tolerances.
- On consolidation, balances are eliminated at the **first common parent**, with differences posted to a **plug (elimination difference) account**.
- Root cause of mismatches: timing, FX rates, missing partner segment, misposted accounts — fix in GL where possible.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'How does FCCS handle currency translation and CTA?',
`- **Exchange rates** entered by period for rate accounts: **Average** and **Ending**; historical rates/amounts via **overrides** for equity and investments.
- P&L translated at average (periodic) rates; balance sheet at ending; equity at historical.
- **FX on opening balances** and **FX on movements** calculated in the **Movement** dimension; the difference flows to **CTA** (OCI).
- **Currency dimension** shows entity currency, translated (parent) currency and adjustments.
- Supports translation methods for hyperinflation with configuration.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'How does ownership management and NCI work in FCCS?',
`- **Ownership settings** per entity and parent: **% ownership**, **% control**, and **consolidation method** — Holding, Subsidiary (full consolidation), Proportional, Equity, Discontinued, etc.
- FCCS calculates **non-controlling interest** for partially owned subsidiaries and performs **investment/equity eliminations**.
- Ownership can change by period (acquisitions, disposals).
- Mention configurable consolidation rules for special cases.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'How do you integrate Fusion GL with FCCS?',
`- **Data Integration (Data Exchange)** with a **Fusion GL source connection**: select ledgers/balance types, periods.
- **Mappings**: GL account → FCCS account; BSV/legal entity → Entity; intercompany segment → Intercompany; currency; optional cost center/product → custom dimensions.
- **Period and category** mappings; **load rules** run per period (scheduled during close).
- **Validation** checks and **drill-through** to Fusion GL balances/journals.
- **Write-back** of FCCS journals to GL is possible but usually not needed.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'What is the Movement dimension and why is it useful?',
`- Captures **roll-forward detail** for balance sheet accounts: opening balance, increases, decreases, acquisitions, disposals, FX on opening, FX on movement, closing.
- Drives the **automated cash flow statement** (indirect method) and roll-forward disclosures (PP&E, equity).
- Requires GL data with movement detail (via mappings or supplemental data) or calculated movements.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'What is the Multi-GAAP dimension used for?',
`- Stores **local GAAP**, **IFRS/US GAAP adjustments** and the **total** for another GAAP within the same application.
- E.g. load local GAAP trial balances, enter or calculate IFRS adjustments, and report IFRS consolidated results.
- Alternative is separate scenarios; Multi-GAAP keeps reconciliations visible.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'How do journals work in FCCS and when would you use them?',
`- **Consolidation journals** for **top-side adjustments**: reclassifications, consolidation entries, IFRS adjustments, late adjustments that shouldn't go back to the local ledger.
- Journals have **workflow** (submit, approve, post), can be recurring, auto-reversing, and are posted to specific Consolidation/Data Source members.
- Good practice: minimize top-side journals; correct at source (GL) where possible for audit trail.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'What is Task Manager / Close Manager in FCCS?',
`- Manages the **close calendar**: tasks, owners, dependencies, due dates, integrations (e.g. a data load task), approvals, and evidence.
- Dashboards show status across entities.
- Helps shorten and control close; used alongside **Account Reconciliation (ARCS)** for balance sheet reconciliations.`, 'B'],

['FCCS', 'What is Supplemental Data Manager and Enterprise Journals?',
`- **Supplemental Data Manager**: collects detailed data not in the GL (e.g. debt schedules, lease details, intercompany transaction detail, disclosure data) via forms with workflow; can feed consolidation accounts.
- **Enterprise Journals**: manage journals with workflow and post them to ERP or FCCS — useful for controlled top-side and recurring entries.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'What does the approval unit hierarchy do?',
`- Defines **who prepares, reviews and approves** each entity's data (by entity/scenario/period), with **promotion** through levels.
- Locks data once approved; prevents changes after sign-off.
- Supports a controlled, auditable close process.`, 'B'],

['FCCS', 'What is the calculation status and why do you care?',
`- Each entity/period shows a status: **OK** (consolidated and current), **Impacted** (data changed since last consolidation), **System Changed** (metadata/rates changed), **No Data**.
- Before reporting, ensure entities are **OK** — rerun consolidation if impacted.
- Common interview trick: "numbers differ between two reports" → check calc status and rates.`, 'B'],

['FCCS', 'How would you design the entity and account hierarchies in FCCS?',
`- **Entity hierarchy** reflects legal ownership (for consolidation/NCI) and possibly alternate hierarchies for management or regions (shared members).
- **Account hierarchy** aligned to financial statements, with **system accounts** FCCS requires (e.g. FCCS_Total Balance Sheet, Income Statement), plus rate and ownership accounts.
- **Map** from the GL COA with clear ownership of mappings.
- Keep custom dimensions minimal for performance.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'How does FCCS produce the cash flow statement?',
`- Uses the **Movement** dimension: for each balance sheet account, movements are tagged (e.g. increases/decreases, acquisitions, non-cash, FX).
- Cash flow accounts are calculated from these movements (indirect method), with FX effect on cash shown separately.
- Requires good movement data from GL mappings or supplemental data.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'What are common FCCS implementation challenges?',
`- **Mapping quality** from GL (accounts, entities, intercompany partner).
- **Intercompany** discipline in source ledgers.
- **Movement data** availability for cash flow.
- **Ownership** complexity and historical rates for equity.
- **Performance** with many custom dimensions.
- **Change management** for finance users moving from spreadsheets or HFM.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'How does FCCS compare to Hyperion Financial Management (HFM)?',
`- FCCS is the **cloud successor** to HFM: prebuilt consolidation logic, dimensions and reports; quarterly Oracle updates.
- **Less custom rules coding** (configurable calculations vs HFM rules files).
- Built-in **close management**, supplemental data and integrations.
- Migration involves redesigning dimensions and rules, not lifting HFM as-is.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'What is the difference between consolidating in Fusion GL and in FCCS?',
`- **Fusion GL**: ledger sets, translation, intercompany balancing, elimination journals — fine for simpler groups.
- **FCCS**: full consolidation engine — ownership and NCI, equity eliminations, multi-GAAP, movement-based cash flow, journals with workflow, close tasks.
- Rule of thumb: many entities, partial ownership, statutory consolidated reporting → FCCS.`, 'B'],

['FCCS', 'How do you reconcile FCCS to Fusion GL?',
`- **Data load validation** reports (totals by entity/account vs GL trial balance).
- **Drill-through** to GL for differences.
- Check **mapping** changes, **unmapped** members, period/category mappings.
- Check **top-side journals** in FCCS (Data Source dimension shows their effect).
- Sign-off per entity in the approval process.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'Explain the Consolidation dimension members.',
`- **Entity Input**: data loaded/entered for the entity.
- **Entity Consolidation / Entity Total**: entity data after entity-level calculations and adjustments.
- **Proportion**: entity data proportionalized by the ownership percentage.
- **Elimination**: intercompany and investment eliminations.
- **Contribution**: the entity's total contribution to the parent (proportion + elimination + contribution adjustments).
Understanding these is key to explaining "why does the parent number differ from the sum of children".`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'How do you handle an acquisition mid-year in FCCS?',
`- Add the entity to the hierarchy with **effective ownership** from the acquisition period.
- Load opening balance sheet at acquisition (fair values, goodwill via journals/supplemental data).
- **Movement**: acquisition movements for balance sheet and cash flow.
- **NCI** if partial ownership.
- Translate and consolidate from the acquisition date; prior periods unaffected.`, 'D'],

['FCCS', 'How do Fusion GL and FCCS work together during month-end close?',
`- GL: subledgers close → GL adjustments, revaluation, intercompany → GL closed/soft-closed.
- **Data load** to FCCS (scheduled task) → entity reviews → translation/consolidation → top-side journals → approvals → reporting.
- **Task Manager** orchestrates both; **ARCS** reconciliations in parallel.
- Late GL adjustments trigger reload and reconsolidation (calc status Impacted).`, 'B'],

['Interview Prep', 'Give a 60-second summary of your GL, IFRS and FCCS experience for this interview.',
`Shape it like this (tailor the facts):
- "I'm an Oracle Financials [your title] with [N] years in EBS and Fusion GL and subledgers."
- "I've designed **multi-ledger** structures with **primary, secondary and reporting-currency ledgers** — for example [your client: industry, number of ledgers, reporting ledgers], and an **IFRS** program for [your client and why IFRS was needed]."
- "I know the **IFRS vs US GAAP** differences that drive ledger and SLA design, and the **close**: revaluation, translation, intercompany, allocations and consolidation in **FCCS**."
- "As a contractor I come in, learn the close quickly, and fix what slows it down."`, 'B'],

['Interview Prep', 'What questions should you ask the client at the end of a GL/IFRS/FCCS interview?',
`- How many ledgers/entities, and which GAAPs do you report under?
- Is consolidation in FCCS today, or moving from HFM/spreadsheets?
- What are the biggest close pain points (days to close, intercompany, top-side journals)?
- Any IFRS 18 or other standard changes in scope?
- What does success look like in the first 90 days of this contract?`, 'B']
]);

QB['ora-mod-gl'] = (QB['ora-mod-gl'] || []).concat([["Enterprise Structures","Design the enterprise structure for a company with 40 legal entities in 15 countries, a US parent reporting in USD under US GAAP.","## Ledgers\n- **Primary ledgers** per country/currency (local currency, local calendar if required), shared **global COA** where possible.\n- **Reporting currency (USD)** for each non-USD primary ledger, or translation in consolidation — reporting currency if daily USD visibility is needed.\n- **Secondary ledgers** only where local GAAP differs materially and is maintained in Oracle; otherwise local adjustments in a statutory tool.\n- **Ledger sets** by region for period management.\n\n## Legal entities and BUs\n- Legal entities with balancing segment values; **BUs** by country or region; **shared services BUs** for AP/AR as service providers.\n\n## Consolidation\n- **FCCS** for group consolidation, eliminations, minority interests.\n\n## Key trade-offs to explain\n- Global COA vs local COA (secondary ledger with COA mapping), number of ledgers vs period-close effort, translation method.","D"],["Enterprise Structures","When would you put multiple legal entities in one ledger and when would you separate them?","**Same ledger** when entities share the 4 Cs (COA, calendar, currency, accounting method) — use **balancing segment values per legal entity**. Benefits: fewer ledgers to close, simpler intercompany, shared reporting.\n\n**Separate ledgers** when any of the 4 Cs differ, or when:\n- statutory reporting needs strict separation or local auditors require it;\n- different close calendars/timelines;\n- data security is easier at ledger level;\n- planned divestiture (cleaner carve-out).\n\nTrade-off: many entities per ledger need careful **intercompany balancing rules** and security by BSV.","D"],["EPM","How do Oracle EPM modules fit with Fusion Financials?","- **Planning (EPBCS)** — budgets and forecasts; budgets can be loaded to GL for budgetary control/reporting.\n- **FCCS** — consolidation and close.\n- **ARCS (Account Reconciliation)** — balance sheet reconciliations and transaction matching.\n- **Narrative Reporting** — management/board reporting packages.\n- **Tax Reporting (TRCS)** — income tax provision.\n- **Profitability and Cost Management** — allocations and profitability.\n- Integration via **Data Integration**: GL balances to EPM (and drill-back to Fusion).\n\nArchitect the dimensions (accounts, entities) consistently across GL and EPM.","D"],["Chart of Accounts","A client wants to add a new segment to the COA two years after go-live. How do you respond?","- **Understand the need**: what reporting is missing? Often it can be met with **hierarchies**, **supporting references**, **DFFs**, projects, or EPM dimensions.\n- If a segment is truly needed: in Fusion you can't simply add a segment to a used COA structure. Options: **new COA + new ledgers** with **COA mapping** and balance migration, or use an unused **future segment** if one was designed in.\n- Impact: integrations, SLA rules, reports, FR, EPM, security, cross-validation rules, user training.\n- Recommend a **business case** and a planned change at a fiscal year boundary.\n\nLesson for designs: always include a **future segment**.","D"],["Chart of Accounts","What makes a chart of accounts \"future-proof\"?","- Clean **segment purposes** (no overloaded segments).\n- Room in value ranges; **future segment(s)** with a default value.\n- **Hierarchies** for reporting instead of hard-coded rollups.\n- **Intercompany segment** from the start.\n- **Global consistency** with controlled local extensions.\n- **Governance** for adding values (owners, approval, naming).\n- Alignment with **EPM dimensions** and consolidation.","B"],["Intercompany","How would you design an end-to-end intercompany solution?","- **Types of flows**: goods (SFO), services/management fees (Fusion Intercompany), financing (loans — treasury), cost allocations (GL allocations with intercompany balancing).\n- **Intercompany segment** in COA and **balancing rules** by legal entity pair.\n- **Invoice vs journal-only** decision per country (tax and statutory need invoices in many countries).\n- **Settlement/netting** process (monthly netting via treasury).\n- **Reconciliation** tooling (Fusion IC reconciliation or ARCS transaction matching).\n- **Elimination** in FCCS.\n- **Transfer pricing** policy aligned with tax.","D"],["Period Close","How would you architect a \"continuous close\" for a large enterprise?","- **Event-driven accounting**: subledgers account and post continuously (scheduled hourly/daily).\n- **Automated reconciliations** (ARCS transaction matching, bank reconciliations daily).\n- **Automated accruals and allocations** with schedules.\n- **Close task management** (FCCS/ARCS) with dependencies and dashboards.\n- **Anomaly detection** (AI) on journals and balances.\n- **Soft close** monthly, hard close quarterly where appropriate.\n- KPIs and continuous improvement.","D"],["Accounting","How do you design SLA rules for a new revenue stream without disrupting existing accounting?","- Identify **event classes** and sources affected.\n- **Copy and version** the accounting method; add new journal line rules/ADRs with conditions specific to the new stream.\n- Use **supporting references** for reporting the new stream.\n- Test with an **accounting matrix** including reversals and adjustments.\n- Activate at a period start; reconcile the first close carefully.","D"],["Enterprise Structures","Design the SCM structure for a company with 5 plants, 12 DCs, central procurement and 3 selling entities.","- **Inventory orgs**: one per plant and DC; item master org.\n- **Cost orgs**: by plant (standard cost) and grouped DCs (average or standard), with **cost books** for primary and management views.\n- **Procurement BU** centralized; requisitioning BUs per operating entity; **sold-to legal entity** rules.\n- **Order management BUs** per selling entity; **fulfillment** from any DC via **sourcing rules**.\n- **SFO flows**: central procurement entity → plants; plants → selling entities (transfer pricing).\n- **Planning**: one global supply plan with all orgs; demand by selling entity/region.\n- **Shipping networks** between DCs; transfer orders for rebalancing.\n\nValidate with finance (legal flows, tax) before configuration.","D"],["General Ledger","What are the \"4 Cs\" of a ledger in Oracle?","A ledger is defined by four things:\n- **Chart of accounts** — the account structure (segments).\n- **Calendar** — the accounting periods (e.g. monthly, 4-4-5).\n- **Currency** — the ledger currency.\n- **aCcounting method** — the Subledger Accounting method that tells subledgers how to create journals.\n\nTwo legal entities can share one primary ledger only if all four are the same; otherwise they need separate ledgers.","B"],["General Ledger","What is a balancing segment and why is it important?","The balancing segment is the chart-of-accounts segment (usually **Company**) for which Oracle ensures debits equal credits on every journal.\n\n- If a journal crosses two balancing segment values (e.g. company 101 and 102), Oracle automatically creates **intercompany balancing lines** using the intercompany balancing rules.\n- Balancing segment values are typically assigned to **legal entities**, so you can produce a balance sheet per entity.\n- Fusion supports up to three balancing segments (primary, second and third) — e.g. Company and Cost Center.","B"],["General Ledger","Walk me through the life of a manual journal entry in Fusion GL.","- **Create**: Journals → Create Journal (or upload via the ADFdi spreadsheet). Enter batch, journal, ledger, period, category, lines.\n- **Validate**: cross-validation rules and account combinations are checked; invalid combinations are rejected.\n- **Approve**: if journal approval is enabled, the journal routes through BPM approval rules (often by amount or source).\n- **Post**: posting updates account balances (GL_BALANCES); can be manual or via AutoPost.\n- **Reverse** (if needed): reversal journals for accruals, scheduled for the next period.\n\nTip: Mention the spreadsheet upload (ADFdi) — most finance teams use it for bulk journals.","B"],["General Ledger","What are cross-validation rules?","Cross-validation rules stop invalid combinations of segment values from being created.\n\nExample: \"Balance-sheet accounts (1000–3999) must use cost center 000.\" The rule has a **condition filter** (when the rule applies) and a **validation filter** (what's allowed), plus an error message.\n\n- They only stop **new** combinations — existing ones must be disabled separately.\n- Set them up early, before transactions start, and test them with real account strings.","B"],["General Ledger","How do you open and close GL periods, and what statuses can a period have?","Period statuses: **Never Opened, Future Enterable, Open, Closed, Permanently Closed.**\n\n- Open the next period with **Manage Accounting Periods** (or the Open Period process).\n- Journals can be entered in Future Enterable periods but not posted.\n- Close the period after all subledgers have transferred and all journals are posted and reconciled.\n- A Closed period can be reopened; Permanently Closed cannot — use it only after audit sign-off.\n\nSubledgers (AP, AR, FA) have their own period statuses, closed before GL.","B"],["General Ledger","What is the difference between a primary ledger, a secondary ledger and a reporting currency?","- **Primary ledger** — the main book of record for a legal entity.\n- **Secondary ledger** — a parallel ledger that can differ in chart of accounts, calendar, currency and/or accounting method. Used for local GAAP vs IFRS/US GAAP or a different COA for statutory reporting. Can be maintained at subledger, journal or balance level.\n- **Reporting currency** — same COA, calendar and method as the primary; only the currency differs. Used when you just need to report in another currency.\n\nRule of thumb: only the currency differs → reporting currency; anything else differs → secondary ledger.","D"],["General Ledger","Explain revaluation and translation in GL.","- **Revaluation** adjusts **foreign-currency balances** (e.g. a EUR bank account in a USD ledger) to the period-end exchange rate. The difference goes to unrealized gain/loss. Typically reversed the next period.\n- **Translation** converts the **whole ledger's balances** into another currency for reporting/consolidation — balance sheet at period-end rate, P&L at average rate, equity at historical rates; the difference goes to the Cumulative Translation Adjustment (CTA).\n\nRun revaluation before translation, after all foreign-currency transactions are posted.","D"],["General Ledger","How would you set up a recurring allocation, for example rent allocated by headcount?","Use **Calculation Manager** allocation rules in Fusion GL:\n- **Source (pool):** the rent account balance for the period.\n- **Basis:** a statistical account holding headcount by cost center (loaded each month).\n- **Target:** rent expense on each receiving cost center, proportional to headcount.\n- **Offset:** a credit back to the source cost center.\n\nGenerate the allocation each month (manually or on a schedule), review, and post. In EBS the equivalent is **MassAllocations**.\n\nTip: Mention you test with a small known set of numbers so you can check the math by hand.","D"],["Period Close","What steps would you follow to close the Payables period?","- Make sure all invoices are entered and **validated**; resolve holds that matter for the period.\n- Complete the **payment runs** for the period.\n- Run **Create Accounting** and transfer to GL; clear any accounting errors.\n- Review the **Unaccounted Transactions** and exception reports.\n- **Sweep** remaining unaccounted transactions to the next period if appropriate.\n- **Close the Payables period**, then open the next one.\n- Reconcile the AP trial balance to GL.","B"],["Period Close","What is the typical order of closing subledgers before GL?","- **Purchasing/Receiving** — receipts complete; accruals.\n- **Payables** — invoices and payments accounted; close.\n- **Receivables** — invoices and receipts accounted; close.\n- **Cash Management** — bank statements reconciled.\n- **Assets** — additions from AP posted; depreciation run; close (Assets needs Payables done first).\n- **Projects/Inventory/Cost** if used.\n- **General Ledger** — post, revalue, translate, consolidate, close.","B"],["General Ledger","What are journal categories and sources used for?","- **Journal source** — where the journal came from (Manual, Payables, Receivables, Spreadsheet, a custom source for an integration).\n- **Journal category** — what kind of entry it is (Accrual, Adjustment, Purchase Invoices, Receipts).\n\nThey are used in **journal approval rules**, **reporting and audit** (e.g. all manual journals), **reversal options**, **AutoPost criteria** and **document sequencing**.","B"],["General Ledger","What is the difference between Smart View and Financial Reporting Studio?","- **Smart View** is an Excel add-in that queries the GL balances cube. Great for ad-hoc analysis, pivoting and building Excel-based reports with live data and drill-down.\n- **Financial Reporting (FR) Studio / Financial Reporting Center** produces formatted, governed reports (P&L, balance sheet) published to many users — though Oracle is moving these to Narrative Reporting and Smart View-based reports.\n\nBoth read from the same **Essbase cube** that is updated when journals post.","D"],["General Ledger","What is the difference between a journal batch, a journal and a journal line?","- **Batch** — a group of journals posted together (e.g. one month's accruals).\n- **Journal (entry)** — belongs to one ledger, period, category and currency; must balance.\n- **Line** — one debit or credit to an account combination, with description and amount.\n\nApproval and posting usually happen at batch level; reversals can be done per journal.","B"],["General Ledger","How do recurring journals work?","- Used for entries that repeat every period with the same or formula-based amounts (rent, amortization, standard accruals).\n- In Fusion, built with **Calculation Manager** formula rules (or the recurring journal option), then **generated** each period and posted.\n- Amounts can be fixed, or calculated from balances (e.g. 1/12 of a prepaid account).\n\nTip: Mention you schedule generation to run right after period open.","B"],["Enterprise Structures","Walk me through how you would design the enterprise structure for a multi-country Fusion Financials implementation.","Start from how the business is legally, managerially and fiscally organized — the structure should mirror that, not the org chart.\n\n## The building blocks\n- **Legal entities** — one per registered company; they own transactions, tax registrations and statutory reporting.\n- **Primary ledgers** — one per unique combination of chart of accounts, calendar, currency and accounting method (the \"4 Cs\"). Countries sharing all four can share a ledger with balancing segment values per legal entity.\n- **Business units** — where transactional work happens (AP, AR, Procurement); assign service-provider relationships if a shared-services center processes for many BUs.\n- **Reference data sets** — share setups (payment terms, customers, suppliers' sites) across BUs instead of duplicating.\n- **Secondary or reporting-currency ledgers** — for local GAAP vs IFRS/US GAAP, or group-currency reporting.\n\n## How I'd approach it\n- Run workshops with finance, tax and legal to confirm entities, currencies and statutory needs per country.\n- Design the chart of accounts with group consolidation in mind first, local statutory needs via secondary ledgers or segment mapping.\n- Prototype in a sandbox with real transactions and the close process before locking it in — this is the hardest thing to change later.\n\nTip: Mention a real example: how many ledgers, why you chose secondary ledgers vs reporting currencies, and one trade-off you made.","D"],["Chart of Accounts","How do you design a chart of accounts for a global company, and what mistakes have you seen?","A good COA answers reporting questions with segments, not with hundreds of natural accounts.\n\n## Design principles\n- **Typical segments:** Company (balancing), Cost Center, Natural Account, Intercompany, Product/Line of Business, Future1 — each with a clear owner and purpose.\n- Keep segments **orthogonal** — one meaning per segment, no \"smart\" codes that encode two things.\n- Leave room: number ranges by account type, spare segments for growth, and a future segment.\n- Use **hierarchies (account and cost-center trees)** for roll-ups and reporting instead of creating summary values.\n- Define **cross-validation rules** early to stop invalid combinations at entry.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Copying the legacy COA one-for-one, including its workarounds.\n- Using cost centers to track projects or customers (use subledger attributes or the right segment).\n- Too many segments — users and integrations suffer; balancing headaches multiply.\n- Ignoring intercompany: no intercompany segment makes eliminations painful.\n\nTip: Interviewers like a \"before vs after\" — e.g. reduced 4,000 natural accounts to 900 by moving detail into segments and hierarchies.","D"],["Period Close","How do you run and improve the month-end close in Oracle Financials?","I treat the close as a sequenced checklist across subledgers with clear owners, cut-off times and reconciliations.\n\n## Typical sequence\n- **Procurement/AP:** complete receipts, run receipt accrual, validate and account invoices, transfer to GL, close AP period.\n- **AR:** finish billing and receipts, run revaluation if needed, account and transfer, close AR period.\n- **Assets:** add assets, run depreciation, create accounting, close FA period.\n- **Cash Management:** reconcile bank statements.\n- **GL:** post journals, run revaluation and translation, intercompany and allocations, reconcile subledger to GL, close the period, consolidate.\n\n## How I improve it\n- Use the Fusion **Close Monitor** and task lists so status is visible to the controller.\n- Move work earlier: daily create-accounting runs, continuous reconciliation, accrual automation.\n- Track a metric (days to close) and remove bottlenecks — on one program we went from 8 to 5 working days by automating accruals and bank reconciliation and moving intercompany settlements to weekly.\n\nTip: Lead with a number — days to close before and after — then explain what you changed.","D"],["Period Close","A CFO wants to shorten the close from 10 days to 5. What would you do?","## Diagnose\n- Map the current close calendar: tasks, owners, dependencies, durations; find the **critical path**.\n\n## Typical levers\n- **Continuous accounting**: daily Create Accounting, daily bank reconciliation, weekly intercompany settlement.\n- **Automate accruals** (receipt accruals, recurring journals, allocations).\n- **Earlier cut-offs** for AP/AR with estimates for the last days.\n- **Auto-reconciliation** of balance sheet accounts (e.g. account reconciliation tools).\n- **Standard close checklist** with task management (FCCS/ARCS or similar) and visibility.\n- Fix data issues upstream (e.g. invalid accounts) so close isn't spent on corrections.\n\n## Result\n- Measure days to close and post-close adjustments; iterate each month.","D"],["Period Close","What is a third-party control account in SLA?","An account flagged so that **only subledger transactions with a third party (supplier/customer)** can post to it.\n- Prevents **manual GL journals** to accounts like AP liability or AR receivables.\n- Enables **third-party balance reporting** (supplier/customer balances) from SLA.\n- Keeps subledger-to-GL reconciliation clean.\n\nSet on the natural account value (Third Party Control Account = Supplier/Customer).","D"],["General Ledger","How do you handle suspense accounts and journal imports from external systems?","- **Journal import** from external sources via FBDI (Journal Import template), GL REST, or OIC.\n- Define a **custom journal source** per system for traceability, approval and reporting.\n- **Suspense posting**: can be enabled to post unbalanced journals to a suspense account — better to keep it off and fix imbalances at source.\n- Validate COA combinations upfront (cross-validation rules) and use **dynamic combination creation** carefully.\n- Monitor import errors daily; reconcile the source system to GL by source.","D"],["General Ledger","What is average daily balancing and when is it used?","**Average balance processing** keeps **daily balances** and calculates average balances (period-to-date, quarter-to-date, year-to-date).\n- Needed by **banks and financial institutions** for regulatory reporting and interest calculations.\n- Enabled on the ledger (average balance processing), with **effective dates** on journals.\n- Fusion supports average daily balances in GL with specific reporting.\n\nMention it only if relevant — it shows breadth.","D"],["Period Close","How would you use Fusion features to automate intercompany and close tasks?","- **Intercompany**: automatic balancing rules; intercompany transactions with auto-invoicing; reconciliation reports.\n- **Allocations** and **recurring journals** in Calculation Manager, scheduled.\n- **Auto-reversal** of accruals; **AutoPost** criteria.\n- **Revaluation/translation** scheduled.\n- **Close monitor** for status; AI agents for close insights where available.","D"],["General Ledger","How do you configure journal approval in Fusion GL?","- Enable **journal approval** on the ledger and on the **journal sources** that require it (e.g. Manual, Spreadsheet).\n- Define the approval rules in **BPM Worklist** (Manage Journal Approval Rules):\n- by journal amount (e.g. over $50k → controller);\n- by ledger or category;\n- by supervisor hierarchy or approval groups.\n- Test edge cases: amounts at the threshold, multiple ledgers, self-approval prevention.\n- Consider whether subledger journals need approval (usually not — they're controlled upstream).","D"],["General Ledger","What is AutoPost and when would you use it?","AutoPost automatically posts journals that match **criteria sets** — by ledger, source, category, period and priority.\n- Used for subledger journals (Payables, Receivables, Assets) and trusted integrations so finance doesn't post them manually.\n- Schedule it to run regularly.\n- Exclude sources that need review or approval (manual journals).\n\nTip: Combine with Create Accounting \"transfer and post\" options to keep GL current daily.","D"],["General Ledger","How do segment value security rules work?","Segment value security restricts which **segment values** a user can see or use — e.g. a regional accountant can only use cost centers in their region.\n- Enable security on the value set, create **data security policies** for value conditions, and grant them through roles.\n- In Fusion, it's managed via **Manage Value Sets → security** and data roles in the Security Console.\n- It affects entry and inquiry/reporting; combine with **data access sets** (ledger/BSV access).\n\nBe careful: too many rules hurt performance and usability.","D"],["General Ledger","What is a data access set?","A data access set controls which **ledgers** (or ledger sets) and which **primary balancing segment values** a user can access in GL, and whether it's read-only or read/write.\n- **Full ledger** access, or **specific BSVs** (e.g. company 101–110 only).\n- Assigned to users through **Manage Data Access for Users** with GL roles.\n- Ledger sets let a user work across ledgers that share COA and calendar (e.g. open/close periods together).","B"],["Intercompany","How does Fusion Intercompany work?","Two parts:\n- **Intercompany balancing** — automatic: when a journal crosses balancing segment values, GL/SLA creates due-to/due-from lines based on **intercompany balancing rules** (by ledger, legal entity, source/category).\n- **Intercompany transactions** — the **Intercompany module**: one entity creates a transaction (e.g. management fee), the partner approves it; it creates journals in both ledgers and optionally **AR invoices and AP invoices** to settle.\n\nAlso set up: **intercompany organizations**, **transaction types**, **receivables/payables assignments**, and the **intercompany reconciliation** report.","D"],["Intercompany","How do you reconcile intercompany balances at month end?","- Run the **Intercompany Reconciliation** report (Fusion) — compares due-to/due-from between entity pairs.\n- Investigate differences: timing (one side booked in the next period), FX rate differences, missing transactions, manual journals with the wrong intercompany segment.\n- Use an **intercompany segment** in the COA so you can report balances by trading partner.\n- Agree a **cut-off date** and settlement process (netting).\n- Eliminations happen in consolidation (FCCS or GL elimination rules).","D"],["Budgetary Control","What is budgetary control and encumbrance accounting?","- **Budgetary control** checks spending against budgets in real time — requisitions, POs and invoices can't be approved if they exceed available funds (absolute control) or warn (advisory).\n- **Encumbrance** reserves funds: requisition encumbrance → PO encumbrance → actual (invoice) — each step relieves the previous.\n- Common in public sector, higher education and some corporates.\n- Setup: control budgets, budgetary control rules, control levels by segment, and loading budgets.\n\nFunds check vs funds reserve: check only validates; reserve consumes funds.","D"],["Period Close","How do you use the Close Monitor and close tasks in Fusion?","- **Close Monitor** shows period status across subledgers and ledgers in one view — which are open, closed, or pending.\n- In Fusion GL, **Period Close** components include the close status per ledger and drill to subledgers.\n- Many companies use **Oracle EPM Financial Consolidation and Close** task manager (or BlackLine) for the close checklist with owners, dependencies and due dates.\n- I set up the checklist to mirror the real sequence, with evidence attached for audit.","D"],["Period Close","How do you handle period-end accruals for received but not invoiced (RNI) goods?","- **Accrue at receipt** (common for inventory and many expense items): the accrual is booked when goods are received and cleared when the invoice is matched.\n- **Period-end accrual** (for some expense items): run the **Create Accrual** / **Accrue Uninvoiced Receipts** process at period end; it's reversed next period.\n- Review the **accrual reconciliation report** for old or wrong accruals; write off with approval.\n- Agree with finance which method applies by item/destination type.","D"],["Multi-currency","How are exchange rates managed and used in Oracle?","- **Rate types**: Corporate, Spot, User, etc.\n- Daily rates are loaded (manually, via spreadsheet, or integration from a provider like Reuters/Bloomberg/a bank).\n- Transactions use the rate for the transaction date and rate type; **User** rate types let users enter a rate.\n- **Revaluation** uses period-end rates; **translation** uses period-end, average and historical rates.\n- Ensure rates are loaded **before** processing — missing rates block accounting.","B"],["Multi-currency","How do realized gains and losses arise in AP and AR?","- When an invoice in a foreign currency is **paid** (AP) or **receipted** (AR) at a different exchange rate than the invoice rate, the difference is a **realized gain or loss**.\n- Example: EUR 1,000 invoice booked at 1.10 ($1,100), paid at 1.12 ($1,120) → $20 realized loss for AP.\n- SLA books it to the realized gain/loss accounts at payment/receipt.\n- **Unrealized** gains/losses come from revaluing open balances at period end.","B"],["General Ledger","How do you design account hierarchies for reporting?","- Build **trees** for the natural account (Assets, Liabilities, Equity, Revenue, Expense, with sub-levels) and for cost centers (department → division → company).\n- Align with the **financial statement lines** and management reporting needs.\n- Create **tree versions** effective by date; **audit** and **flatten** them, then **publish** to the Essbase cube.\n- Use the same hierarchies in allocations, FR reports and Smart View.\n- Govern changes (who can add nodes; change control).","D"],["General Ledger","What is a ledger set and why use one?","A ledger set groups ledgers that share the **same chart of accounts and calendar**, so you can:\n- open/close periods for many ledgers at once;\n- run revaluation, translation, allocations and reports across them;\n- grant access with one data access set.\n\nLedgers with different currencies can still be in a set. Very useful for companies with many country ledgers.","B"],["General Ledger","How do you handle a journal posted to the wrong period?","- If the wrong period is still open: **reverse** the journal (reversal in the same period) and re-enter it in the correct period.\n- If the wrong period is closed: reverse in the current open period (or reopen if policy allows) and post correctly; document for audit.\n- Check if allocations, revaluation or reports already used the wrong balance.\n- Fix the cause (default period on spreadsheet uploads, user training).","B"],["Accounting","Explain how you would design accounting for a complex revenue arrangement with variable consideration.","- **ASC 606 / IFRS 15**: estimate variable consideration (rebates, usage, penalties) using expected value or most likely amount; **constrain** to avoid significant reversal.\n- In Oracle: **Revenue Management** for allocation and recognition; variable consideration via **estimates** (adjustments to transaction price) and true-ups as actuals arrive.\n- **Rebates/chargebacks**: channel revenue management or accruals via SLA/GL.\n- **Accounting**: contract liabilities/assets, revenue, accrued rebates.\n- **Controls**: estimates reviewed quarterly with documented methodology.\n- Reporting for disclosures (RPO, disaggregation).","D"],["Accounting","How do you handle Pillar Two / global minimum tax data requirements in the ERP landscape?","- Pillar Two (15% global minimum tax) needs **jurisdictional data**: GloBE income, covered taxes, deferred taxes, by constituent entity.\n- **ERP role**: clean legal entity structure, entity-level trial balances, mapping of accounts to GloBE adjustments, intercompany detail.\n- Calculation typically in **Tax Reporting (TRCS)** or specialized tax tools fed from GL/FCCS.\n- Architect **data lineage** and controls: tax data is audited too.\n- Partner early with the tax department; changes to COA or entity mapping may be needed.","D"],["Accounting","How would you design accounting for intercompany loans and interest across entities?","- **Treasury** owns loan agreements (in a TMS or Fusion with custom process).\n- **Interest accruals** monthly by agreement (rates, day-count) → journals in both entities (lender interest income, borrower expense) with **intercompany segment**.\n- **FX revaluation** of foreign-currency loans; consider **net investment** treatment where appropriate.\n- **Withholding tax** on interest across borders.\n- **Settlement** via netting or cash.\n- **Reconciliation** and elimination in FCCS.\n- Transfer pricing documentation (arm's-length rates).","D"],["Accounting","How would you support IFRS 17 or similar complex accounting standards with Oracle?","- Calculations (CSM, risk adjustment, cohorts) usually in **actuarial/specialized engines**.\n- **Fusion Accounting Hub** to turn calculation outputs into accounting under IFRS 17 and local GAAP (multiple methods/ledgers).\n- **Data lineage** and reconciliation from policy systems → engine → FAH → GL.\n- **Granular dimensions** (portfolio, cohort) via supporting references rather than COA segments.\n- **Disclosure** reporting from a data warehouse.\n\nEven outside insurance, the pattern applies to any complex standard: specialized calculation + FAH + GL.","D"],["Accounting","What is your approach to multi-ledger, multi-currency design for treasury-heavy companies?","- **Functional currency** per entity determined with accounting (ASC 830/IAS 21).\n- **Reporting currencies** where daily group-currency balances are needed.\n- **Revaluation** rules by account range (monetary items only), with gain/loss accounts by entity.\n- **Hedge accounting** handled in treasury systems with journals to GL (cash flow hedge reserves in OCI).\n- **Translation** in FCCS with CTA.\n- Daily rates feed from a market data provider; controls on rates.","D"],["Accounting","How do you approach a request to restate prior-period accounting in Oracle?","- Confirm the **accounting decision** (finance/audit) and periods affected.\n- Options: **adjusting entries in the current period** (most common), or **reopening prior periods** (if allowed and not reported) with corrections.\n- For subledger errors: correct the source if possible and re-account; otherwise manual journals with references.\n- **Revaluation/translation** and consolidation reruns for affected periods.\n- **Audit trail** and documentation; control to prevent recurrence.","D"],["Accounting","How would you design accounting controls around AI-assisted close tasks?","- AI prepares: **accrual proposals**, **reconciliation match suggestions**, **variance explanations**.\n- Humans review/approve; postings go through normal **journal approval**.\n- **Evidence**: agent outputs and traces attached to close tasks.\n- **Thresholds**: materiality-based review levels.\n- **Testing** of AI-assisted controls by internal audit.","D"]]);
